CAT = "uksiaComms"; SEG = "COM"
# Unit 1, LO7, LO8 and LO11: communication, customer care, record keeping, post-incident.
Q = [
("Which is an example of non-verbal communication?",
 "Facial expression", ["Tone of voice", "A text message", "A written report"],
 "Non-verbal communication includes gestures, stance, eye contact and facial expression. Tone and pitch belong to verbal communication; texts and reports are written."),
("Why do security teams use the NATO phonetic alphabet on the radio?",
 "To spell words clearly", ["To keep messages secret", "To save battery life", "To meet licensing law"],
 "The phonetic alphabet lets you spell names and registrations without being misheard and gives quick call signs. It is not a code and does not hide the message."),
("In the NATO phonetic alphabet, which word stands for D?",
 "Delta", ["David", "Dog", "Dover"],
 "D is Delta. The full alphabet runs Alpha, Bravo, Charlie, Delta, Echo and so on."),
("In the NATO phonetic alphabet, which word stands for K?",
 "Kilo", ["King", "Kate", "Kent"],
 "K is Kilo, between Juliett and Lima. Using the standard words avoids confusion between similar sounding letters."),
("A customer complains about a long wait. What is the best first step?",
 "Acknowledge them and listen", ["Send them to the back", "Ask a colleague to remove them", "Say it is not your job"],
 "Good customer service starts by acknowledging the customer and listening to find out what they need. Then explain, take responsibility and involve them in the solution."),
("Which of these is an internal customer for a door supervisor?",
 "The venue's bar manager", ["A customer in the queue", "A member of the public", "A passing police officer"],
 "Internal customers are people within the organisation or client you work for, such as venue managers and bar staff. People in the queue are external customers."),
("Why is accurate record keeping important?",
 "You need not rely on memory", ["It replaces the need for CCTV", "It is needed to renew a licence", "It means you never give statements"],
 "Records give a clear audit trail, help you comply with the law and mean you are not relying on memory months later in court."),
("You make a mistake in your pocket notebook. What should you do?",
 "Cross it out once and initial it", ["Tear out the page and start again", "Cover it with correction fluid", "Scribble over it until it is unreadable"],
 "Draw a single line through the error so it can still be read, and initial it. Removing pages or hiding errors makes the record look altered and weakens it as evidence."),
("When should you write up an incident?",
 "As soon as possible", ["At the end of the week", "Only if the police ask", "Before your next shift"],
 "Notes made at the time or as soon as possible afterwards are the most accurate and carry the most weight as evidence."),
("Which details should an incident record include?",
 "Who, what, when, where and how", ["Only the names of anyone ejected", "Your opinion of those involved", "The bar takings for the night"],
 "A good record says who was involved, what happened and what was done, when, where and how, plus witnesses, injuries and property. Stick to facts, not opinions."),
("What is a section 9 statement?",
 "A witness statement for court", ["A licence application form", "A risk assessment record", "An SIA warning letter"],
 "A section 9 statement is a written witness statement that can be used as evidence in criminal proceedings. Security staff may be asked to give one after an incident or arrest."),
("You are asked to give evidence in court about an incident. What should you do?",
 "Be punctual and prepared", ["Agree a story with colleagues", "Bring the venue's CCTV system", "Refuse unless you are paid"],
 "Follow your employer's procedures and any legal advice, read your notes beforehand and arrive on time. Your evidence must be your own account, not one agreed with others."),
("Why is it important to get support after a serious incident?",
 "It reduces the risk of lasting harm", ["It is needed to keep your SIA licence", "It stops any police investigation", "It decides who was to blame"],
 "Support after an incident lowers the risk of depression, anxiety and post-traumatic stress, and helps you reflect on what happened."),
("Which of these is a source of post-incident support?",
 "A helpline such as Samaritans", ["The person you ejected", "The local licensing committee", "The SIA licensing team"],
 "Support can come from colleagues, managers, counsellors, helplines such as Samaritans, Citizens Advice and trade unions."),
("What is one benefit of reflecting on incidents as a team?",
 "It helps stop problems recurring", ["It decides who loses their job", "It replaces written reports", "It ends any police inquiry"],
 "Reflection identifies good and poor practice, spots trends and improves procedures so the same problem is less likely to happen again."),
("Which is good practice for protecting your own personal information?",
 "Keep social media accounts private", ["Wear your work uniform on the way home", "Post photos from work online", "Share rota details with friends"],
 "Security staff can be targeted, so manage privacy settings, do not wear anything identifiable outside work and do not discuss work information outside the workplace."),
]
